====== Gfg  N meetings in one room (28th Feb)=======
Step-by-step algorithm to find the maximum number of meetings that can be held in a room:
Step 1: Create a 2D array arr where each element stores a meeting’s start and end time.
•	Sub-step 1a: For each meeting, store start time in arr[i][0].
•	Sub-step 1b: Store end time in arr[i][1].
Step 2: Sort the meetings in ascending order based on their end times.
•	This ensures that we always consider the earliest finishing meeting first.
Step 3: Initialize count = 1.
•	The first meeting can always be scheduled.
Step 4: Initialize ed to the end time of the first meeting.
•	This keeps track of the last meeting’s end time.
Step 5: Traverse the rest of the meetings from the second meeting onward:
•	Sub-step 5a: If the start time of the current meeting is greater than ed,
o	Increment count by 1.
o	Update ed to the end time of the current meeting.
(This means we can schedule this meeting without overlap.)
•	Sub-step 5b: If the start time is less than or equal to ed, skip this meeting.
(It overlaps with the last selected meeting.)
Step 6: After checking all meetings, return count.
Time Complexity:
•	Sorting meetings → O(n log n)
•	Traversing meetings → O(n)
Total = O(n log n)
Space Complexity:
•	Storing meetings in a 2D array → O(n)
•	Other variables → O(1)
Total = O(n)



           -----------> Code <-------------

class Solution {
    // Function to find the maximum number of meetings that can
    // be performed in a meeting room.
    public int maxMeetings(int start[], int end[]) {
        int[][] arr=new int[start.length][2];
        
        for(int i=0;i<start.length;i++){
            arr[i][0]=start[i];
            arr[i][1]=end[i];
        }
        
        Arrays.sort(arr,(a,b)->Integer.compare(a[1],b[1]));
        
        int count=1;
        int ed=arr[0][1];
        for(int i=1;i<start.length;i++){
            if(arr[i][0]>ed){
                count++;
                ed=arr[i][1];
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
